<Docs
	page="recipes/elixir"
	title="Elixir"
	lede="Heredocs and ~S sigils passed to Ecto or Exqlite, via tree-sitter-elixir loaded from its release."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Loading the grammar</h2>
		<p>
			squill doesn&rsquo;t compile Elixir in, so the rule names the grammar by
			its release URL. The first run downloads it and records its SHA-256 in a{" "}
			<code>squill.lock</code> beside the config; commit that, and run CI with{" "}
			<code>squill fmt --locked</code>. A loaded grammar has no default query,
			so every rule brings one. See{" "}
			<a href="docs/embedded/#grammars">bringing your own grammar</a> for how
			both work.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			squill only rewrites a string from a loaded grammar that already spans
			lines: in Elixir, a <code>"""</code> heredoc, a <code>~S"""</code> sigil,
			or a plain string written over several lines. A one-line string is left as
			written.
		</p>
		<p>
			A string&rsquo;s escapes come back exactly as written. One with
			interpolation (<code>#&lbrace;&rbrace;</code>) is SQL with a hole in it,
			and the queries below skip it; pass values as parameters instead. The same
			goes for sigils: only <code>~S</code>, which never interpolates, is taken.{" "}
			<code>~S"""</code> is the natural choice for a migration, since
			backslashes in it are literal; the queries{" "}
			<a href="docs/embedded/#properties">tell squill so</a>, and it formats one
			holding a backslash.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Ecto</h2>
		<p>
			<code>Repo.query!</code>, <code>Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query!</code>, and a
			migration&rsquo;s <code>execute</code>, in the default Postgres dialect:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="elixir-ecto" lang="elixir" />
		<p>
			Ecto records which migrations have run by version, not by content, so
			reformatting an old one is safe. With <code>ecto_sqlite3</code>, set{" "}
			<code>dialect = "sqlite"</code> in the rule.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>Exqlite</h2>
		<p>
			<code>Exqlite.Sqlite3</code>&rsquo;s <code>execute</code> and{" "}
			<code>prepare</code>, in the SQLite dialect:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="elixir-exqlite" lang="elixir" />
	</section>
</Docs>
